---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How to Become an Oil Rig Welder — Certifications & Career Path"
  description="The complete path to becoming a rig welder: required certifications, process qualifications, and career progression from junior welder to CWI inspector."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Welding', href: '/welding' }, { label: 'Certifications', href: '/welding/certifications' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/welding/certifications"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/welding/certifications" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Welding — Career Path</div>
    <h1>How to Become a <span>Rig Welder</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">The certifications that actually matter, and the realistic progression from junior welder to specialist or inspector.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;">Career <span>Path</span></h2>
        <div class="career-path">
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Junior Welder <span class="inline-badge badge-entry">Entry</span></h3>
            <div class="cs-meta"><span class="cs-years">0–3 years</span><span class="cs-salary">$45K–$65K/yr</span></div>
            <p>Foundational welding skills, basic offshore safety training (BOSIET). Limited to supervised repairs. Working toward AWS CW certification.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Journey Welder <span class="inline-badge badge-mid">Mid</span></h3>
            <div class="cs-meta"><span class="cs-years">3–7 years</span><span class="cs-salary">$65K–$95K/yr</span></div>
            <p>Multiple process qualifications (SMAW, GTAW, FCAW). API 1104 and AWS D1.1 certified. Works independently on structural and pipeline work.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Senior / Specialist Welder <span class="inline-badge badge-senior">Senior</span></h3>
            <div class="cs-meta"><span class="cs-years">7–12 years</span><span class="cs-salary">$90K–$130K/yr</span></div>
            <p>All-position pipe welding, exotic alloy experience. Interprets engineering drawings and works from WPS without supervision.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Underwater Welder / Diver <span class="inline-badge badge-expert">Expert</span></h3>
            <div class="cs-meta"><span class="cs-years">5+ yrs combined</span><span class="cs-salary">$80K–$150K+/yr</span></div>
            <p>Combined commercial diver and welder. See the <a href="/welding/underwater-welding">full underwater welding guide</a>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="career-step">
            <h3>Welding Inspector (CWI) <span class="inline-badge badge-expert">Expert</span></h3>
            <div class="cs-meta"><span class="cs-years">5+ yrs + AWS exam</span><span class="cs-salary">$80K–$140K/yr</span></div>
            <p>AWS Certified Welding Inspector verifies weld quality and approves procedures. Consistent demand regardless of oil price cycles.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;">Certifications <span>Explained</span></h2>
        <div class="card-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>AWS Certified Welder (CW)</h3>
            <p>Entry-level performance qualification testing your ability to produce sound welds in specific positions and processes. The starting credential for most rig welding jobs.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>API 1104</h3>
            <p>Pipeline welding standard. Tests welding in multiple positions (including the demanding 6G pipe position) using procedures qualified to this code. Essential for pipeline work.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>AWS D1.1</h3>
            <p>Structural steel welding code. Required for platform, derrick, and structural fabrication and repair work.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>ASME Section IX</h3>
            <p>Governs welding procedure and welder qualification for pressure vessels and piping systems — required for high-pressure vessel work on rigs.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>AWS D3.6M</h3>
            <p>The underwater welding code, covering both wet and hyperbaric welding qualification classes (A, B, and O).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>AWS CWI</h3>
            <p>Certified Welding Inspector — requires welding experience plus a rigorous exam. Moves you from welding to inspecting and approving others' work.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Test Positions</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">What 1G Through <span>6G</span> Actually Mean</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:740px;margin-bottom:24px;">Welder qualification is defined by <strong style="color:var(--white);">position</strong>, not by process or appearance. The position you test in determines the positions you are qualified to weld in production — which is why 6G is the one worth having.</p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Position</th><th>Pipe or Plate</th><th>What it means</th><th>Qualifies you for</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1G</td><td>Pipe, rolled</td><td>Pipe rotates while you weld; you stay in the flat position throughout.</td><td>Flat only</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2G</td><td>Pipe, vertical</td><td>Pipe axis vertical, weld runs horizontally around it. Fixed, no rotation.</td><td>Flat, horizontal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5G</td><td>Pipe, horizontal fixed</td><td>Pipe axis horizontal and fixed. You weld overhead, vertical and flat in a single joint as you travel round.</td><td>Flat, vertical, overhead</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6G</td><td>Pipe, 45° fixed</td><td>Pipe fixed at 45°. Every position in one weld, with no comfortable place to start. The hardest of the four.</td><td>All positions</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6GR</td><td>Pipe, 45° with restriction ring</td><td>6G with a plate obstructing access, simulating welding next to structure you cannot move.</td><td>All positions, restricted access</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note-box mt-24" style="max-width:800px;">
      <strong>Why employers ask for 6G.</strong> Passing 6G qualifies you for every position below it, so one test covers the range. On a rig, joints are wherever the pipework happens to be — you cannot rotate a mud line to suit yourself. A 6G ticket says you can weld the joint in the position you find it.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">The Paperwork</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">WPS, PQR and <span>WPQ</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:740px;margin-bottom:20px;">Three documents that get confused constantly, and that any inspector will ask for. They answer three different questions.</p>

    <div class="card-grid grid-3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>WPS</h3>
        <p><strong>Welding Procedure Specification.</strong> The recipe: process, material, joint design, filler, amperage range, preheat, travel speed. It tells you how the weld is to be made. It is a document about the <em>procedure</em>, not about you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>PQR</h3>
        <p><strong>Procedure Qualification Record.</strong> The evidence that the recipe works — a test weld made to the WPS and then destructively tested. One PQR supports the WPS. It proves the <em>procedure</em> is sound.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>WPQ</h3>
        <p><strong>Welder Performance Qualification.</strong> The evidence that <em>you</em> can execute the WPS. This is your ticket, tied to position, process, material group and thickness range. It is what expires.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;max-width:740px;margin-top:22px;">The practical consequence: turning up with a valid WPQ is not enough on its own. The job needs an approved WPS backed by a PQR for the material and joint in question, and your qualification has to fall within its range. A ticket in carbon steel does not carry over to duplex, and a qualification on 10&nbsp;mm wall does not automatically cover 25&nbsp;mm.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Keeping It</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Continuity and <span>Renewal</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;max-width:740px;">A welder qualification is not a permanent award. Under most codes it stays valid only while you keep using the process — commonly a six month continuity window, after which an unused qualification lapses. Employers usually maintain a continuity log for exactly this reason, and it is worth keeping your own copy rather than relying on a former employer's records.</p>
    <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;max-width:740px;">Requalification means retesting. That is time and money, so a hand who moves between contractors is generally better off keeping tickets live on the processes they actually intend to work, rather than collecting qualifications they will let lapse.</p>
    <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;max-width:740px;">Test costs vary by region and test house, and are frequently paid by the employer once you are on a contract — but rarely before. Budget for funding your own first ticket, and treat any employer who pays for requalification as a reason to stay.</p>

    <div class="note-box" style="max-width:800px;">
      <strong>Offshore survival note:</strong> In addition to welding certs, offshore welders require BOSIET (helicopter underwater escape, sea survival, fire fighting) and HUET — see the <a href="/careers/certifications" style="color:var(--amber);">full certifications guide</a>. Welders performing structural hot work also need Hot Work Permit awareness and confined space entry training.
    </div>

    <div class="related-links" style="max-width:900px;">
      <a href="/welding" class="related-link"><div class="rl-label">Overview</div><div class="rl-title">Rig Welding Processes</div></a>
      <a href="/welding/underwater-welding" class="related-link"><div class="rl-label">Specialism</div><div class="rl-title">Underwater Welding</div></a>
